				\begin{document}$$\pm$$\end{document}±1.5 standard deviations (s.d.) relative to the piControl. As a result, their occurrence frequency increases by a factor of 3 to 4, from about 5 events per century after 2100 (Supplementary Fig. 6), indicating that these salinity extremes involve both enhanced amplitude and increased frequency. To examine the mechanism driving this intensification, we performed a composite analysis of extreme events identified after 2100 (see Methods). Figure 2a shows the spatial evolution of upper-ocean salinity anomaly from –6 to +2 years relative to the peak. Positive salinity anomaly originating at the western boundary propagates eastward over 6 to 8 years, amplifying as it travels across the North Atlantic basin. The anomaly center tends to shift southward from the eastern North Atlantic toward the subtropics, while certain signals appear to propagate poleward along the European coastline, possibly by the mean surface circulation. In addition, negative anomalies also propagate eastward, both preceding and following the positive anomaly.Fig. 2Emergent extreme salinity mode over the North Atlantic.
a Spatial evolution of MME-mean composited extreme upper-ocean salinity event from Year ﻿–6 (top) to Year +2 (bottom). The composite is constructed from events identified during 2100–2299. Dots represent statistically significant at the 95% confidence level. b Composited time evolution of the upper-ocean salinity anomaly over the black rectangle region in Fig. 2a. c Salinity tendency from salinity budget analysis (see Methods). Black line is a tendency of Fig. 2b and gray dashed line is sum of all budget terms. Green line is the sum of the horizontal advection terms. Magenta line is the anomalous advection of climatological meridional salinity gradient. Blue is same as magenta but using piControl climatology for sensitivity test. Red dots represent a statistically significant at the 95% confidence level.

The North Atlantic climate is projected to undergo dramatic changes in future warming. The Gulf Stream weakens and spatial salinity contrasts become more pronounced (Supplementary Fig. 12), both of which are key to explaining the emergence and amplification of salinity mode, as demonstrated in the idealized model. These mean state changes are consequences of the AMOC weakening15,26. Multi-model comparison reveals that models exhibiting a stronger AMOC decline tend to show a significant weakening of upper-ocean current speed over the Gulf Stream (Fig. 4a). Also, the AMOC weakening enhances the negative meridional gradient of climatological salinity, especially along the midlatitude (Fig. 4b). This effect is most pronounced in the central North Atlantic, where the amplitude of salinity variability also becomes largest. The mechanism is physically consistent: as the weakened Gulf Stream reduces the northward transport of warm and salty subtropical waters, both the spatial contrasts in salinity and temperature are intensified. These relationships are robust across models, with high inter-model agreement (Fig. 4c).Fig. 4Inter-model relationships among change in AMOC, mean states, and variability.
a, b Inter-model correlation maps between the changes in AMOC strength and the upper-ocean (a) current speed and (b) meridional salinity gradient. Dots represent statistically significant at the 95% confidence level. c Inter-model relationship between the changes in the current speed over the Gulf Stream (white box region in a) and the meridional salinity gradient over the central North Atlantic (black box region in a). Each marker represents an individual model. d Inter-model relationship between changes in the current speed over the Gulf Stream and the standard deviation (s.d.) of upper-ocean salinity over the central North Atlantic. e Inter-model relationship between changes in the meridional salinity gradient and standard deviation of upper-ocean salinity anomaly over the central North Atlantic. The change (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
