The extended SSP5-8.5 scenario represents the worst-case pathway in which global warming continues unabated. To evaluate the possibility of avoiding the emerging salinity extremes by CO2 mitigation, a set of idealized CO₂ ramp-up and ramp-down experiments are conducted (black curve in Fig. 5a; see Methods). In the ramp-up phase, CO₂ follows a similar evolution as the extended SSP5-8.5 scenario, reaching a peak at four times the present-day level. The ramp-down phase then mimics idealized mitigation scenario by gradually reducing CO₂ level.Fig. 5Compound salinity and SSH extremes impacting coastal Europe even under CO2 mitigation.
a Time evolutions of CO2 removal experiment. black line is CO2 forcing in CO2 ramp-up and ramp-down scenarios. Gray line is historical-SSP5-8.5 scenarios, shown for reference. Red line is change in \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
